llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
114): the decode step of a Gated DeltaNet convolution, with its state read
from and written to the cache, runs as one kernel.
Generic (all GPUs): not gfx803-specific code; measured on gfx803.

WHY
---
Single-token decode on an RX 590 pays for every dispatch as well as for the
work in it: each one costs about 4.3 us of command-processor time however
small the kernel, a 2.65 us floor plus 1.65 us for the vector-L1 invalidate
that every dispatch needs on gfx8 (docs/FINDINGS.md, "A kernel can read
stale data from the vector L1 across dispatches"). A Qwen3.6 35B-A3B decode
token with the routed experts in system RAM issues about 1,425 dispatches,
about 1,064 of them on the critical path.

At one token the causal convolution of a Gated DeltaNet layer (Qwen3.5/3.6,
Qwen3-Next) is four dispatches: GET_ROWS copies the conv state (d_conv - 1
= 3 columns per channel) out of the cache, CONCAT appends the token's
column of the qkv projection, CPY stores the last 3 columns of the window
back into the cache, and SSM_CONV (with SILU fused) convolves the 4-column
window. None touches more than 128 KiB (8192 channels x 4 columns of f32 on
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B), so the chain is almost all dispatch cost, and one kernel
can do all of it: 90 dispatches per token fewer on Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (30
DeltaNet layers).

Alone, with HIP graphs off (tg128 x5, two rounds): 26.30 -> 27.00 tok/s (+2.7%).

Measured on an RX 590 (llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in
system RAM with -ncmoe 99, tg128, 5 repetitions, page cache dropped before
each run, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1 ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1), patches 113-116
together: 25.60 -> 27.51 tok/s with HIP graphs (the default), 26.21 ->
27.93 tok/s with GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1. With this patch alone and with
all four, greedy 128-token output is byte-identical to the build without
them, and llama-perplexity with -ub 1 (every token through the decode path)
gives mean KLD 0 and the same perplexity in every digit.

WHAT
----
For one sequence and one token, ggml_cuda_try_defer_state_rows matches the
conv-state GET_ROWS whose RESHAPE feeds a dim-0 CONCAT(state, new input),
together with the CONCAT's two users: the view of its last d_conv - 1
columns that a CPY stores into the same cache, and SSM_CONV followed by
SILU. The plan is recorded in the context's conv_step_plans, and GET_ROWS,
CONCAT and CPY are not run. At the SSM_CONV, ssm_conv_step_silu_f32 (one
thread per channel) reads the channel's state from cache row ids[0] and its
new input, writes the shifted window to the CPY's cache row, and computes
the convolution and SILU with the expressions and summation order of
ssm_conv_f32 at one token. When both rows are the same row, the in-place
update is safe because a thread writes only values it has already read.
The match refuses when any node in between writes the cache, the row ids
or the new input, or reads the cache, and when the SILU output overlaps
anything the kernel reads; graph_optimize keeps the row ids and the new
input allocated until the SSM_CONV. d_conv 3 or 4, f32 only, main stream
only; every other case runs the four kernels as before, and
GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_FUSION=1 turns this off.

Numerics are unchanged: same expressions and summation order as
ssm_conv_f32 (the taps compile to the same v_mac sequence on gfx803), no
new fma.

